I cannot confirm any current link-down fault. End-to-end reachability between the endpoints is healthy: bidirectional pings succeed with 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT (pc0↔pc12). Both hosts have correct default gateways (pc0 via 20.1.1.1 on r1; pc12 via 120.1.1.1 on r12).

The core routing control plane is up. On r1, OSPF neighbors are Full with r3 over eth2 (C, 31.1.1.0/8) and with r11 over eth1 (B, 111.1.1.0/8). Interfaces on r1 (eth1, eth2, eth4) are UP with valid addresses (111.1.1.1, 31.1.1.2, 20.1.1.1). On r11, the OSPF neighbor to r1 over eth1 is Full, and r11 has EIGRP-learned routes toward r12 over eth0 (EE, 112.1.1.0/8). On r12, the LAN toward pc12 (eth1, 120.1.1.1) and the uplink to r11 (eth0, 112.1.1.2) are UP, and routing is in place (default via 112.1.1.1; 120.0.0.0/8 connected).

Mechanistically, if an interior link had failed, we would expect OSPF neighbor loss, missing routes to 120.0.0.0/8 on r1, or failed pings. None of these are observed; all adjacencies and interfaces are up and traffic passes.

Root cause: No active backbone link failure is present; the earlier outage was either transient during the maintenance window or not reproducible now.

Corrective fix: No action required. Recommend closing the ticket and, for post-mortem, review interface/OSPF logs and counters on r1/r11/r12 during the cited window and enable monitoring to capture future transient events. 
